{
  "teamId": 137,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 San Francisco Giants were a team searching for an identity, bouncing between flashes of genuine promise and the sobering reality of a roster caught between its championship past and an uncertain future. They stumbled out of the gate in March and April, going a combined 12-18 while their bullpen — led by the mercurial Reyes Moronta — repeatedly turned leads into losses in the game's most crucial moments. Early on, aging veterans like Pablo Sandoval and Evan Longoria were asked to carry a lineup that simply didn't have enough pop, and the results were predictably inconsistent.\n\nMay brought a brief exhale, as clutch hits from Joe Panik, Buster Posey, Stephen Vogt, and Brandon Crawford gave the club a sense that veteran savvy might be enough to keep them competitive. But the Giants never strung together enough sustained excellence to build real momentum, and a 10-16 record in May reflected a team spinning its wheels. June was marginally better at 14-13, providing just enough hope to keep the fanbase invested as summer approached.\n\nThen came July — the most improbable month of the San Francisco season. The Giants caught fire, going a stunning 19-6, fueled by a Pablo Sandoval renaissance, the electric emergence of rookie Mike Yastrzemski, and a team that suddenly couldn't stop scoring. It was the kind of stretch that makes you believe in magic, and for a few glorious weeks Oracle Park hummed with the energy of contention. But baseball is a long game, and August and September told a different story — the bullpen cracked, opponents hit towering home runs off San Francisco relievers at the worst possible moments, and the Giants faded to 11-16 in each of those final months.\n\nThe 2019 Giants finished 77-85, missing the playoffs and landing in the middle of a franchise crossroads. The season was ultimately defined by that extraordinary July — a reminder of what this group could be on its best days — sandwiched between months of struggle that made clear the road back to October would require something more than nostalgia and clutch moments from familiar faces.",
